# audit/identity-allowlist — CONF-10 durable handling
#
# Each non-empty, non-# line records ONE permitted residue exception:
#
#   <path-glob>\t<token-name>\t<reason>
#
#   path-glob   fnmatch-style glob against the scanner's relative path
#               (relative to fork/source for the S1 fork audit, relative
#               to the staging tree for the S3 ISO audit).
#   token-name  IDENTITY_TOKENS key (see scripts/identity_scan.py), or
#               '*' to allow every token at this path.
#   reason      Free-text rationale.  Surfaces when this entry
#               suppresses a finding.  Be specific.
#
# Categories (kept in this order):
#   1. Legal / policy retention — copyright, changelog, upstream READMEs.
#   2. Non-shipped fork metadata — debian/control prose, source comments.
#   3. Dormant network-install surface — choose-mirror entire tree, kept
#      in source for future re-activation but not exercised today.
#   4. Operator-facing fork docs — README.md describing the fork itself.
#
# When the audit fires on a new file, decide: is it (a) actual leakage
# to fix, or (b) a category-1..4 exception?  Add a line here only for (b).

# ── 1. Legal / policy retention ────────────────────────────────────────
*/debian/changelog	*	upstream changelog history (legally required to retain)
*/debian/copyright	*	upstream license attribution (legally required)
*/debian/README	*	upstream package README retention
*/debian/README.*	*	upstream package README variants
*/licenses/*	*	upstream license texts
*/copyright	*	upstream copyright file retention

# ── 2. Non-shipped fork metadata ───────────────────────────────────────
# debian/control Description / Maintainer fields surface only in dpkg-query;
# operator never sees them on the installed system at runtime.
*/debian/control	Debian	descriptive prose in package Description (XSBC-Original-Maintainer + fork prose)
*/debian/control	debian.org	XSBC-Original-Maintainer / Original-Maintainer (Debian Policy fork convention)
# Build-system source code comments — never shipped to installed system.
*/apt-setup-verify	debian.org	source-code comment about apt-setup upstream history
*/generators/*	Debian	apt-setup generator source-code comments (build-time only)
*/generators/*	debian.org	apt-setup generator source-code comments (build-time only)

# ── 3. Dormant network-install surface (choose-mirror) ─────────────────
# CD-only mode is the default today (project_self_contained_repo).
# choose-mirror remains forked for future re-activation; its templates
# + mirrorlist describe a Debian-style mirror infrastructure that's not
# exercised under the current build.  Re-audit this allowlist block if
# `[Build] EnableNetworkInstall = true` ever lands.
choose-mirror/*	*	network-install fork — dormant in CD-only mode; rebrand pending re-activation
choose-mirror/debian/*	*	choose-mirror upstream debian/* metadata
choose-mirror/README.Athena	*	fork-specific operator README (intentional upstream comparison)

# ── 4. Operator-facing fork docs (README.md describing the fork) ───────
*/README	Debian	operator-facing fork README — discusses upstream Debian relationship
*/README.md	Debian	operator-facing fork README — discusses upstream Debian relationship
*/README.md	debian.org	operator-facing fork README — discusses upstream Debian relationship
*/README.FHS	*	upstream FHS-policy README retention
*/TODO	*	upstream TODO file retention

# ── 5. athena-tasksel pod / perl tasksel source ────────────────────────
# tasksel.pod is operator-facing man-page material discussing how
# tasksel relates to upstream Debian.  tasksel.pl is the perl driver
# with Debian references in code comments.
athena-tasksel/tasksel.pod	*	tasksel pod source — upstream-comparison docs
athena-tasksel/tasksel.pl	*	tasksel perl driver — code comments
athena-tasksel/tasksel.po	*	tasksel translation source

# ── 6. athena-installer-data ramdisk descriptive control ───────────────
athena-installer-data/debian/control	Debian	description ("static data files needed by the Debian Installer ramdisk")

# ── 7. athena-branding 50-athena.cfg (live-config descriptors) ─────────
athena-branding/data/50-athena.cfg	*	upstream live-config script that documents Debian variants

# ── 8. Vendor identity files + system-info templates ───────────────────
# base-files/origins/debian is the dpkg-vendor identity file that
# explicitly declares Asgard as a Debian-derivative; the filename and
# `Parent-Vendor: Debian` content are structural, not branding.
base-files/origins/debian	*	dpkg-vendor parent-distro declaration (structural)
# info dir template — surfaces in `info` browser; TODO rebrand prose in
# a follow-up.  Comment-line "In Debian #OSNAME#…" is template text,
# not exposed prose.
base-files/share/info.dir	Debian	system info dir template — rebrand TODO (low-visibility)

# ── 9. Test fixtures + internal source comments ────────────────────────
athena-tasksel/tests/*	*	tasksel test fixtures — comment text, not shipped
athena-pkgsel/pre-pkgsel.d/20update-policy	Debian	source-code comment about upstream default behavior
athena-tasksel/debian/rules	Debian	build-rules source comment (not shipped)
athena-tasksel/README.translators	*	translator docs — points to DDTP (upstream translation resource)

# ── 10. Top-level operator-facing fork READMEs ─────────────────────────
README.md	*	operator-facing fork-tree README — documents fork rationale

# MAT-03 — booted-root (squashfs chroot) scan carve-outs.  Upstream
# attribution and licensing texts legally RETAIN the Debian name; the
# invariant targets OUR identity surfaces (os-release, menus, debconf,
# boot), not upstream provenance.  Same rationale as the fork
# debian/copyright entries above.
usr/share/doc/*	*	upstream attribution/changelogs — legally retained
usr/share/common-licenses/*	*	license texts — verbatim by definition
usr/share/man/*	*	upstream manpages document upstream reality

